-- A STUN binding server as its own deployment: `drt --config this start`.
--
-- Run TWO of these on separate addresses (the stun1/stun2 pair). One
-- server tells a peer what address it saw; two tell it whether that
-- address CHANGED between vantage points, which is the fact that decides
-- whether hole punching can work at all. A client asking only one server
-- gets an address; a client asking two gets to know whether to trust it.
--
-- Unlike the relay and the http listener, this is meant to face the world
-- — reporting the address the world sees is the entire service — so bind
-- accordingly, and put it behind the same rate limiting as anything else
-- that answers strangers.
return {
  supervisor = "stun_supervisor.lua",

  stun = {
    bind = "0.0.0.0",
    port = 3478,        -- the IANA STUN port
    queue = "stun_in",  -- where counters land on the supervisor
    report_ms = 10000,  -- how often; the server is stateless, so this is
                        -- a heartbeat, not an event stream
  },
}
